## Phase 2: The Architect 
### Chapter 4: Intelligence Gathering 

Chapter 4 focuses on Intelligence Gathering and how literature is deeper than we initially believe it to be. The idea that stood out to me the most in this chapter was that a literature review should map a scholarly conversation instead of simply summarizing a list of sources. Before reading this chapter I believed literature reviews were to prove that I had found enough articles based on my topic. The conversation metaphor helped me better understand why that approach is incomplete. In a literature review I need to first understand what researchers already agree on, whether they disagree, and which questions remain unanswered, then I can contribute my own ideas. 

This chapter applied to my project about how social media and other platforms track their users interests to place personalized content and ads based on their previous activity and interactions. When researching my topic, I could easily find many separate articles based on algorithms, data collection, targeted advertising, or filter bubbles. However, this method would not effective explain how their findings relate. A stronger review could group these sources into themes and sub themes, such as information platforms collect, how recommendation algorithms use that information, and how personalization affects what users see. Organizing research in this way would make it easier to identify patterns and disagreements.

I was also interested in the section about how research gaps do not always mean that nobody has studied that topic. A gap can involve limitation in the methods used or a contradiction in findings. For example, many studies may ask users how they feel about personalized content, but their answers might not match their actual online behavior. Comparing self-reported opinions shows me that the goal is not to collect the largest number of sources. But the goal is to search systematically and combine sources into an argument that clearly explains why my research is needed.
